    {#
      ======================================================================
      HOME PAGE

      TEN SECTIONS, AND NO TWO OF THEM ARE BUILT THE SAME WAY. The page this
      replaces was five variations on "eyebrow, big heading, grid of cards",
      which made every screen read like the one before it. Each section here
      has one job, and a structure only it uses. The reasoning for each is in
      homepage.css, next to the rules that draw it, along with the argument for
      the running order.

      NO SECTORS SECTION, AND FOUR SERVICE ROUTES RATHER THAN FIVE. Both are
      the client's own instruction of 11/08/26: "Remove Sectors completely" and
      "Services, remove Flush & Precomm". Those pages 301 in src/_redirects, so
      a card offering pre-commission cleaning would have sent the one visitor
      who wanted it to a page about boreholes. The coverage map now stands
      where the sectors list did, answering "do you come to me" directly.

      ROUTING SITS THIRD, one screen from the fold, because on a page selling
      systems that cost seven figures the visitor almost always arrives already
      knowing something has to be sorted. The positioning statement reads
      better after the five questions than in front of them: by then it is
      answering "why does this have to be done properly" rather than opening
      with it.

      THREE OF THE TEN ARE THE SITE'S OWN COMPONENTS, unchanged and included
      rather than reimplemented: the coverage map that closes /about/ and every
      service page, the filmstrip from /about/, and the enquiry drawer the
      whole site shares. A homepage that drew its own map or its own photo band
      would be a second answer to a question the site already answers once.

      THERE IS NO DEDICATED EMERGENCY SECTION, and that is deliberate. The
      number is already persistent on every screen: the sticky bar carries it
      above 900px, the floating dock carries it below, the hero states it in
      full, and the footer repeats it. An 88px billboard of it in the middle of
      the page was the one block here saying a fifth time what nothing on the
      page lets you miss.

      THE FOOTER CTA IS TURNED OFF ON THIS PAGE (hideFooterCta above), and
      that is not an oversight: the enquiry section IS that call to action, word for
      word, and with both on the page the same heading and the same paragraph
      appeared twice within one screen of each other. The footer itself is
      untouched.

      THE TOOLTIP COMPONENT is site-wide and NOTHING IS MARKED UP HERE. The
      eight hand-written `data-tooltip` spans this page used to carry are gone:
      the `gloss` filter in .eleventy.js now wraps the first occurrence of every
      term in src/_data/glossary.js, on every page, and enforces the same rules
      by machine (never in a heading, never inside a link, two per paragraph,
      once per page). Two of the hand-written ones sat inside a card <a>, which
      the rules said not to do and only a person could miss. To gloss a new
      word, add it to the glossary; to change a definition, change it there.
      ======================================================================
    #}

    {#
      Organization + LocalBusiness, IN THE BODY RATHER THAN THE HEAD, because
      base.njk owns <head> and is shared by every page. JSON-LD is valid and
      indexed anywhere in the document, so the placement costs nothing.

      FOLLOW-UP: base.njk already emits a minimal LocalBusiness on every page.
      Both nodes describe the same business, so give that one the same @id as
      the node below and the two merge into one instead of reading as two
      businesses at one address. That edit belongs to a shared-layout task,
      not to this page.
    #}
    <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@graph": [
        {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization",
          "name": "{{ site.name }}",
          "legalName": "{{ site.legalName }}",
          "url": "{{ site.url }}/",
          "telephone": "{{ site.tel }}",
          "email": "{{ site.email }}",
          "description": "Bespoke water treatment, private water supplies, chlorination and water hygiene compliance for estates, plant and commercial facilities across Surrey, Sussex and the South East.",
          "address": {
            "@type": "PostalAddress",
            "streetAddress": "{{ site.address.street }}",
            "addressLocality": "{{ site.address.locality }}",
            "addressRegion": "{{ site.address.region }}",
            "postalCode": "{{ site.address.postcode }}",
            "addressCountry": "GB"
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "LocalBusiness",
          "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#localbusiness",
          "parentOrganization": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization" },
          "name": "{{ site.name }}",
          "url": "{{ site.url }}/",
          "telephone": "{{ site.tel }}",
          "email": "{{ site.email }}",
          "priceRange": "££",
          "address": {
            "@type": "PostalAddress",
            "streetAddress": "{{ site.address.street }}",
            "addressLocality": "{{ site.address.locality }}",
            "addressRegion": "{{ site.address.region }}",
            "postalCode": "{{ site.address.postcode }}",
            "addressCountry": "GB"
          },
          "areaServed": [{% for c in maps.counties %}{ "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "{{ c }}" }{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}]
        }
      ]
    }
    </script>

    {# ===================== 1 — HERO =====================
      Full screen, left-aligned, and only two things to do: book the survey, or
      call the emergency line. The photograph is a client's terrace at sunset,
      looking out over the hills (L Mill, Brecon), held right back under two washes, so the first screen is TYPE with a
      place behind it rather than a picture with type on it.

      The sticky emergency bar and nav above are untouched, so the number stays
      in view for the whole page; the outlined button here is the same number
      said once at full size, for someone who has landed mid-crisis.
    #}
    <section class="section hp-hero" id="hero" aria-labelledby="hp-hero-title">
      {# THREE FRAMES, CROSSFADING. One still picture behind the one screen
         everybody sees; three says the range of the work without asking anyone
         to click. All real jobs: the mill at dusk, a Tudor estate with the main
         being laid across its lawn, and an Effingham drive with the van, a
         telehandler and the chamber open.
         Only the first is fetched eagerly, so the extra two cost nothing on
         first paint. The fade and the timer live in hero-media.js, and with no
         JS (or with reduced motion asked for) the first frame simply stays.
         aria-hidden on the whole stack: it is scenery, and the h1 beside it
         already says what the page is. #}
      <div class="hp-hero__media" aria-hidden="true" data-hero-media>
        <img class="is-active" src="/assets/l-mill-1879.webp" alt="" width="1536" height="1024" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
        <img src="/assets/b-estate-7931.webp" alt="" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        <img src="/assets/lk-effingham-8595.webp" alt="" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      </div>
      <div class="container">
        {# The line under this one carries the sharpest phrase on the site,
           "properties where getting it right is a legal requirement", and the
           H1 above it was a promise any competitor could make. This states the
           discomfort instead, which is the beat the page was missing and the
           one thing both audiences share: an estate off the mains and a
           facilities manager with an ACOP L8 duty are both answering for water
           nobody else will. Alternatives, if this reads too blunt:
             "The water is yours to answer for."
             "When the water is your responsibility." #}
        <h1 class="hp-hero__title" id="hp-hero-title">Nobody else is responsible <span class="em">for your water.</span></h1>
        <p class="hp-hero__lead">Bespoke treatment, chlorination and hygiene for properties where getting it right is a legal requirement. Designed, installed and maintained personally, across Surrey, Sussex and the South East.</p>
        <div class="hp-hero__actions">
          <a class="btn-inquiry btn-inquiry--lg" href="/enquiry/">Book a site survey <span class="btn-inquiry__arrow" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a>
          <a class="btn-line btn-line--lg" href="tel:{{ site.tel }}" aria-label="Call the 24-hour emergency line on {{ site.phone }}">24hr emergency: {{ site.phone }}</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--light hp-intro" id="about-invicta" aria-labelledby="hp-intro-title">
      <div class="container hp-intro__inner">
        {# An h2 wearing the eyebrow style. The section needs an accessible name
           and this is the only short string in it: the paragraph below runs to
