        <h2 class="eyebrow hp-intro__eyebrow" id="hp-intro-title">Who we are</h2>

        <p class="hp-intro__lead">Invicta is run, day to day, by <strong>Stefan Massingham MSc</strong>, a water chemist of thirty years, and every problem that reaches us is reviewed by him personally. We take care of the science and the compliance, so you are left with a supply you never have to think about.</p>

        <a class="btn-line hp-intro__more" href="/about/">More about Invicta <span class="btn-line__arrow" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a>

        {# THE PROOF SITS UNDER THE CLAIM, in the same centred column, rather than
           in a band of its own. It was its own light section directly below this
           one, which put two light strips back to back with a full section step
           of empty cream between them and made the logos read as an orphan.

           Alpha, Carel, DIS, IAC and SE Water are NOT slotted below. They were
           listed "if licensed", and a client mark goes up when we know we may
           use it, not before. Add a slot each once that is confirmed.
           Accreditation marks are the second row, also awaiting files. #}
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ===================== 2b — TRUSTED BY =====================
      THE REAL BAND, NOT A HAND-BUILT ROW. What stood here was six typed words
      in dashed boxes, slots held until artwork arrived. The client artwork
      arrived in 0cbb6c9 and every other page has shown the marks since; this
      page, the one that matters most, was the last still showing text. Rather
      than drop the files into the old row, this is the component the service
      pages and /gallery/ already use: nine marks, monochrome, moving.

      ON PAPER, not light. .hp-intro above is section--light, and two pale
      sections running together read as one long one.

      THE "ACCREDITED BY" ROW IS NOT HERE. It was CHAS and UKAS as two more
      dashed text boxes, and there is still no artwork for either. Both claims
      are made in words in CREDENTIALS below ("CHAS accredited" and
      "UKAS-accredited laboratory testing"), so nothing is lost by waiting.
      When the marks land they want their own small row, not this marquee: an
      accreditation is not a client.
    #}
    {% set clientsId = "home-clients" %}
    {% set clientsTitle = "Trusted by" %}
    {% set clientsClass = "clients--compact" %}
    {% set clientsSurface = "section--light" %}
    {% include "clients.njk" %}

    {# ===================== 2b — THE PROBLEM =====================
      THE BEAT THE PAGE DID NOT HAVE. It ran hero, then who we are, then a list
      of services, which only lands on a reader who has already decided they
      have a problem. The H1 names the discomfort in one line and nothing
      carried it any further; meanwhile the page's actual pain lines were in
      the CREDENTIALS list at the very bottom ("Section 80 notice? Speak to us
      first", "the rules that will catch you out if you get it wrong"), doing
      no work down there.

      This is not a new claim. The servicing page already says a fault goes
      unnoticed until the water changes or a test fails; this says it where a
      reader is still deciding whether to care, and covers both audiences at
      once — the estate whose supply simply stops, and the duty-holder whose
      building fails an inspection months after the fact.

      Navy, off the back of the light intro above it, so the colour change does
      the separating. The hairline that would normally open a second navy block
      belongs to SERVICES below, which is the section it introduces.
    #}
    <section class="section hp-problem" aria-labelledby="hp-problem-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-problem__inner">
          <h2 class="statement hp-problem__line" id="hp-problem-title">The water only gets your attention <span class="em">when it goes wrong.</span></h2>
          <p class="hp-problem__body">A private supply fails at the borehole, not at a call centre. A building's water fails a test months after the problem started. Either way, the first you hear of it is a bill, a notice, or a property with no water in it, and by then the cheap fix has passed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ===================== 3 — SERVICES =====================
      ROUTED BY PROBLEM, NOT LISTED BY SERVICE. Every heading here is the
      question the visitor arrived with, because someone who has just been
      served a notice does not search for "water hygiene", they search for what
      has gone wrong. The service name is on the page they land on.

      Set as an asymmetric ruled spread rather than a card grid: five equal
      tiles would say these are five equivalent choices, and they are not.
    #}
    <section class="section hp-services" id="services" aria-labelledby="hp-services-title">
      <div class="container">
        {# Two navy sections in a row now, so the rule is what says a new one
           has started. Same job it does under the hero on a service page. #}
        <div class="rule hp-services__rule"></div>
        <p class="eyebrow hp-eyebrow">Services</p>
        <h2 class="statement hp-services__title" id="hp-services-title">What do you need to sort?</h2>

        <div class="hp-services__grid">
          {# THE FLAGSHIP CARRIES THE PHOTOGRAPH, and the four beside it do not.
             Five pictures in a routing grid is five things to look at before
             you can read a single question, and the questions are the point.
             One picture on the cell that already spans two rows is what makes
             the spread read as a magazine page with a lead image rather than
             as a tile grid, and it says which of the five is the flagship
             without a word of copy. Same frame as every other photograph on
             the site: rounded, clipped, drifting on scroll. #}
          <a class="hp-svc" href="/services/private-water-supplies/">
            <span class="hp-svc__media parallax">
